abstract: >
  An interactive browser-based simulation of a particle in a one-dimensional
  finite potential well, designed as a teaching tool for undergraduate
  chemistry. The tool extends the sibling project Particle, Quo Vadis?
  (infinite-well case) by adding the well-depth control V0, a finite count
  of bound states, wavefunction leakage past the walls, and a threshold
  above which both the classical and quantum particles are reported as
  ionised. The central chemistry narrative in the first tab is ionisation:
  the system below V0 is a bound molecular orbital, above V0 is a
  photoejected electron. A second tab compares two independently configured
  finite wells side by side in real units (nm, m_e, eV) with a particle
  preset menu and per-parameter linking between the two systems, supporting
  the geometry- and inertia-driven energy-ladder story relevant to
  conjugated pi-systems, cyanine dye chain-length shifts, and quantum-dot
  fluorescence tuning.
version: 0.2.0
date-released: 2026-05-22
